1
00:00:02,610 --> 00:00:08,170
Many friends have asked me about welding, whether to use tack welding, seam welding,

2
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:09,330
or pulse welding.

3
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:13,590
How to weld a bead that is strong, beautiful, and free from deformation.

4
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,930
In this lesson, I'll share some welding tips with you.

5
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:18,170
Preparation.

6
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:20,210
We need to prepare the following items.

7
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:22,130
First, a welding table.

8
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:27,410
Besides, welding suit, safety goggles, mask, work pieces, and gloves.

9
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:28,410
Step 1.

10
00:00:28,410 --> 00:00:31,010
Set the shielding gas in accordance with this table.

11
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:33,850
For welding, you can use nitrogen or argon.

12
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:35,010
Here I'm using nitrogen.

13
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:38,210
Next, I'll show you how to check the actual gas pressure.

14
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:40,210
First, select machine status.

15
00:00:40,210 --> 00:00:44,650
Then, press and hold the grip sensing button and trigger to allow gas flow.

16
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:47,250
This value is the actual gas pressure.

17
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:48,250
Step 2.

18
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,970
Check whether the lens protector is dirty or damaged, which may decrease the output

19
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:54,290
power and affect welding quality.

20
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:55,290
Step 3.

21
00:00:55,290 --> 00:01:00,010
Ensure that the welding nozzle scale matches the recommended value in the system settings.

22
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:06,090
On the touch screen, tap system settings, and take down the value of focus scale reference.

23
00:01:06,090 --> 00:01:07,090
Open the fastener.

24
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:09,730
Keep consistent with focus scale reference.

25
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:11,290
Close the fastener.

26
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:12,290
Step 4.

27
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:14,810
Install the proper welding wire according to the table.

28
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:20,800
The larger the weld seam, the larger the wire diameter needed.

29
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,480
Also, check whether the wire material matches the workpiece according to this table.

30
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,480
Step 5.

31
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,680
Make sure the wire is installed in the groove of the welding head, otherwise, reinstall

32
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:32,800
it.

33
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,000
Step 6.

34
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,800
Before welding, you need to clean the weld seam.

35
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:39,040
Clean off the oil and dust on the surface.

36
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:43,800
You can clean it using cotton swabs or lint-free cloth with water or alcohol, grind it with

37
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:46,800
a grinder, or use the cleaning nozzle to clean it.

38
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,160
Clean the area to be welded to avoid affecting welding quality.

39
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:52,400
After it dries, you can start welding.

40
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,960
On the touch screen, select the welding mode.

41
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:56,960
There are three welding modes.

42
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,160
Select advanced mode, and you can see the three welding modes.

43
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:04,880
The first is seam welding, the second is pulse welding, the third is tack welding.

44
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:06,840
Let's introduce tack welding.

45
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,400
Tack welding is to use a brief burst of laser emission to weld at a specific point, creating

46
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,440
small, dot-like welds.

47
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:17,640
It's usually used before seam welding and pulse welding as a temporary fixing point.

48
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:21,680
A fixing point, as the name suggests, fixes the workpieces together.

49
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:26,200
For example, tack weld these two points first, then do seam welding.

50
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,400
These two tack welds are the fixing points.

51
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:33,120
Or for some thin items, for example, a one to two millimeter pentagram shaped metal

52
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,120
plate.

53
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:37,200
To fix it onto this round plate, you need tack welding.

54
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,560
Tack welding is very suitable for thin metal materials.

55
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:45,160
Advantages, high precision, low deformation, and a neat appearance.

56
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:49,120
Disadvantage, low load bearing strength, and not very firm.

57
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,400
That's where tack welding is typically used.

58
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,360
Next, we need to set tack welding parameters on the touch screen.

59
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:00,920
After confirming the parameters are correct, turn on enable lasering and perform tack welding.

60
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:04,840
Next, I'll use the pentagram as an example for tack welding.

61
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:08,200
First, roughly fix the position of the pentagram.

62
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,780
Since tack welding doesn't require welding wire, so just align the welding head's red

63
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:21,440
spot to the exact spot and start welding.

64
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:24,640
We can also demonstrate tack welding with metal sheets.

65
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:28,640
First, still align the welding head's red spot with the weld joint.

66
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:34,770
Next, start welding.

67
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:39,850
If blackened marks appear, wipe them clean with alcohol or water.

68
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:44,170
After welding, don't forget to turn off enable lasering on the touch screen to prevent

69
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:45,990
accidental laser emission.

70
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:50,530
We can see many settings available in tack welding mode, including parameters like laser

71
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:52,370
power and spot duration.

72
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:54,890
Adjust them based on welding experience.

73
00:03:54,890 --> 00:03:59,250
If you want stronger penetration in tack welding, you can increase the power value.

74
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:02,970
If you want a nicer look and don't need it to be that strong, you can reduce the

75
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:04,250
power value.

76
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:08,730
Tack time is the same, you can increase or decrease it.

77
00:04:08,730 --> 00:04:12,610
You can also enter professional mode for finer parameter adjustments.

78
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:15,830
Next, let's introduce seam welding.

79
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:20,490
Seam welding is to use high energy continuous laser beams to weld the workpiece continuously

80
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:24,650
and evenly, thus forming a continuous uninterrupted weld bead.

81
00:04:24,650 --> 00:04:29,090
Seam welding is widely used where high strength and heavy load bearing are required, or for

82
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:34,370
airtight connections such as water pipe welding and automobile chassis cross beam welding.

83
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:41,050
Advantages, high weld quality, strong load capacity, no gas leakage, and no liquid leakage.

84
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:46,230
Disadvantages, high cost, thin plates can be burned through easily, large heat affected

85
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:48,770
zone, and risk of deformation.

86
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:51,690
Next, switch to seam welding mode.

87
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:57,210
Use the default parameters, turn on enable lasering, and enable wire feeding to start

88
00:04:57,210 --> 00:04:58,210
welding.

89
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:01,210
Next, we'll demonstrate seam welding.

90
00:05:01,210 --> 00:05:05,930
Because seam welding has a large heat affected zone, thermal expansion and contraction can

91
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:07,130
easily occur.

92
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:12,290
Therefore, when you need to weld a 90 degree angle, set the welding angle a bit larger,

93
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:18,290
for example 100 degrees, then the actual welded angle will be 90 degrees.

94
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:22,610
Tack welding doesn't need this because the heat isn't enough to cause thermal expansion

95
00:05:22,610 --> 00:05:25,550
slash contraction and angle error.

96
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:28,670
Before seam welding, we need to tack the weld points.

97
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:33,830
If the workpieces are long, it's recommended to add a few more fixing points in the middle.

98
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:37,590
When welding fixing points, we don't need to switch to tack welding mode.

99
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:40,510
We only need to turn off this switch on the welding head.

100
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:43,670
Then the welding head won't keep feeding wire automatically.

101
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:47,110
This allows quick switching between seam welding and tack welding.

102
00:05:47,110 --> 00:05:50,070
First, use tack welding to make the fixing points.

103
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:54,210
When switching back to seam welding, remember to turn the switch back on, then it will

104
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:57,790
feed the wire automatically.

105
00:05:57,790 --> 00:06:00,110
Turn on the switch on the welding head.

106
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:03,270
Before seam welding, be sure to check if the position is correct.

107
00:06:03,270 --> 00:06:05,990
You can still make fine adjustments at this time.

108
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:08,770
After seam welding is finished, it can't be adjusted.

109
00:06:08,770 --> 00:06:12,750
Then weld from the start point to the end point without stopping midway.

110
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:15,870
Do not let the wire deviate from the welding path.

111
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:20,270
During welding, do not pull the welding head backward by hand, as the welding wire will

112
00:06:20,270 --> 00:06:23,310
automatically drive the welding head to move backward.

113
00:06:23,410 --> 00:06:36,300
Otherwise, it may cause weld breaks or uneven seam filling.

114
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:40,020
If the welding result isn't good, you can adjust the welding parameters.

115
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:45,700
For example, to make it more penetrative or stronger, we can increase the laser power.

116
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:50,260
If you want less yellowing or blackening, you can reduce the power.

117
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:52,340
You can also adjust the welding width.

118
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:57,260
According to the actual welding needs, laser frequency and wire feeding speed can both be

119
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:58,260
adjusted.

120
00:06:58,260 --> 00:07:02,280
The impact of different parameters refer to the link in the comments section.

121
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:05,280
Next, we'll introduce pulse welding.

122
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:08,600
A fish scale weld bead is made by pulse welding.

123
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:13,160
Pulse welding is to use pulsed laser beams to instantly melts the weld area and forms

124
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:15,560
dot-shaped molten pools.

125
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:19,800
Multiple dot-shaped molten pools overlap one after another, eventually forming a fish

126
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:21,720
scale weld bead.

127
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:27,320
Pulse welding is mainly used for thin plates that need both adequate strength and low deformation.

128
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:31,420
It is a transition process between tack welding and seam welding.

129
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:35,940
Advantages, less deformation and stronger load-bearing performance.

130
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:38,980
It can also handle highly reflective materials.

131
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:43,420
The bead is attractive, needs no grinding, and suits irregular seams.

132
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:48,620
Disadvantages, tiny gaps may exist, resulting in weaker sealing, and it is not as strong

133
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:50,020
as seam welding.

134
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:52,680
Next, switch to pulse welding mode.

135
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:54,580
Use the default parameters.

136
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:57,960
For pulse welding parameter adjustment, there is one thing to note.

137
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:02,920
Since the fish scale appearance of pulse welding is related to the actual welding speed, the

138
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:13,020
parameters above were adjusted based on a typical operating speed and are provided for reference.

139
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:14,020
Enable lasering.

140
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:16,940
Next, we'll demonstrate pulse welding.

141
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:20,960
If you want welding without deformation, and you don't want to reserve extra angle space

142
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:25,540
during positioning, pulse welding can also be done directly without adding wire, so you

143
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:28,920
don't need to consider thermal expansion and contraction.

144
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:41,410
This simplifies the process, and the remaining steps are the same as seam welding.

145
00:08:41,410 --> 00:08:43,510
After welding, you can check the backside.

146
00:08:43,510 --> 00:08:47,470
If there are no marks or deformation on the back, the weld is successful.

147
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:50,830
Next are general welding tips that apply to any mode.

148
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:54,270
First, do not weld directly on the welding table.

149
00:08:54,270 --> 00:08:59,230
Otherwise, the workpiece may get welded onto the table and be impossible to remove.

150
00:08:59,230 --> 00:09:01,990
The weld area must be raised off the table.

151
00:09:02,050 --> 00:09:06,790
Second, the welding nozzle should be at a 45 degree angle to the seam.

152
00:09:06,790 --> 00:09:11,130
It shouldn't be too high or too low, otherwise the success rate will drop.

153
00:09:11,130 --> 00:09:14,090
Third, aim the welding head at the workpiece.

154
00:09:14,090 --> 00:09:18,810
Make the wire tip touch the workpiece, and the red spot fall on the weld position.

155
00:09:18,810 --> 00:09:22,410
Then hold the grip sensing button and trigger to start welding.

156
00:09:22,410 --> 00:09:26,990
Fourth, lift the welding head immediately after finishing to prevent wire sticking.

157
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:31,350
If the welding wire sticks to the workpiece, do not pull the welding head hard or shake

158
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:32,470
it left and right.

159
00:09:32,470 --> 00:09:35,750
This will bend the wire and affect the next weld quality.

160
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:40,250
The correct method is to turn off the wire feeding enable switch on the welding head, then

161
00:09:40,250 --> 00:09:43,930
fire the laser once more while lifting the welding head.

162
00:09:43,930 --> 00:09:51,620
Fifth, if high hardness isn't required and you want a nice seam without black edges,

163
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:55,800
in settings you can reduce the material thickness by 1 to 2 millimeters.

164
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:59,680
For example, if the plate is 2 millimeters thick, choose 1 millimeter.

165
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:02,060
This makes the seam bright with no black edges.

166
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:06,040
If black edges already appear, use the cleaning nozzle to clean it.

167
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:10,680
Sixth, if there is no laser output during welding, it is very likely because the safety

168
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:15,000
interlock loop is not closed or enable lasering is not turned on.

169
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,080
These are safety mechanisms designed to ensure safe welding.
